🔹 Ternary Operator in Java (?:)
The ternary operator is a short-hand replacement for an if–else statement.
It evaluates a condition and returns one of two values based on whether the condition is true or false.

condition ? expression_if_true : expression_if_false;

int a = 10, b = 20;
int min = (a < b) ? a : b;
System.out.println(min); // Output: 10

Q: Can ternary operator replace if-else completely?
A: ❌ No. It is suitable only for simple conditional assignments, not for complex logic or multiple statements.

📊 Comparison: if-else vs Ternary
Feature	if-else	Ternary
Lines of code	More	Less
Readability	Better for complex logic	Best for simple logic
Returns value	No	Yes



